* pk_durable_read.dts -- AIF-156 two-run harness, RUN 2 of 2.
*
* A SEPARATE PROCESS. IT NEVER DECLARES THE KEY. That omission is the entire
* experiment: if the designation lives only in unique_registry.cpp's static map
* it died with run 1, and the REPLACE below succeeds in silence. If it lives in
* the x64 header where the owner ruled it should, the reader picks it up at USE
* and the write is refused.
*
* PKD_T1 IS THE DISCRIMINATOR AND IT READS A FIELD, never console text. If EMPNO
* still reads 1 the write was refused; if it reads 2 it was not. There is no
* third answer and no message to misparse.
*
* THE GUARDS ARE NOT DECORATION. A marker over a CLOSED area does not error --
* it prints a VERDICT, and the polarity depends on the OPERATOR: measured
* 2026-08-28, (SID >= 1) and (LNAME > "") both read .T. over an area with
* nothing open, while (SO_ID = 6) reads .F. So the house rule COUNT THE MARKERS
* does not catch this one: these markers do not vanish, they print and they lie.
* Here (EMPNO = 1) over a table that never opened would read .F. and be reported
* as a durability failure when the real failure was the fixture. PKD_G1 reads
* LNAME, which run 1's PKD_W4 already proved, so a red guard means the table is
* not there and PKD_T1 is UNPROVEN rather than failing.
*
* The finding is written up under the Claude project namespace rather than in
* this repository, so it is DESCRIBED here rather than cited: a path a reader
* of this tree cannot open is worse than no path at all.
*
* ERASES THE FIXTURE. Run 1 deliberately leaves it behind; cleanup is here so a
* second execution of the pair starts from nothing rather than adopting.

SET TALK OFF
STOP_ON_ERROR OFF

SETPATH DBF D:\code\ccode\dottalkpp\data\DBF\SANDBOX
SETPATH INDEXES D:\code\ccode\dottalkpp\data\INDEXES\SANDBOX
SETPATH LMDB D:\code\ccode\dottalkpp\data\LMDB\SANDBOX

SET ALTERNATE TO D:\code\ccode\dottalkpp\data\tmp\pkdur_run2.alt
SET ALTERNATE ON

FORMULA "PKDUR-RUN2-BEGIN"

SELECT 1
USE PKDUR
GO 1

* PKD_G1 -- the fixture survived run 1 and this is the row we think it is.
? "PKD_G1_fixture_reopened:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "ALPHA")

* PKD_G2 -- and it carries the key run 1 minted, before anything tries to
* change it. Without this, PKD_T1 reading 1 cannot distinguish "the write was
* refused" from "the write succeeded and happened to land on 1".
? "PKD_G2_key_survived_as_1:" + (EMPNO = 1)

* NO SET UNIQUE HERE. THIS IS THE POINT.

* PKD_T1 -- the arm. Record 2 already holds EMPNO 2, so this is an attempt to
* duplicate a primary key from a process that never declared one.
REPLACE EMPNO WITH "2"
? "PKD_T1_primary_survived_restart:" + (EMPNO = 1)

* PKD_T2 -- and it survived the write to DISK rather than only in the buffer.
* A refusal that let the value through and merely failed to flush would read
* green above and red here.
CLOSE
USE PKDUR
GO 1
? "PKD_T2_still_1_after_reopen:" + (EMPNO = 1)

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* AIF-158 -- DOES KEY GENERATION READ THE SAME AUTHORITY KEY ENFORCEMENT READS?
*
* THE ARMS ABOVE MEASURE ENFORCEMENT ONLY. A green PKD_T1 says that THIS
* process -- which declared nothing -- refuses a write to EMPNO, so
* field_constraints.cpp reached unique_reg::primary_field(), which asks
* A.primaryFieldIndex() and therefore THE FILE. Generation is a different call
* chain and it was never measured: append_support.cpp::finalize_appended_record
* reaches unique_reg::list_unique_fields(), which reads the process map and
* NEVER OPENS THE HEADER. If that reading is right, a fresh process ENFORCES a
* key it will not MINT, and an appended row takes a blank primary key that the
* engine then refuses to let anyone fill.
*
* THIS BLOCK IS THE SECOND ATTEMPT AND THE FIRST ONE WAS FOOLED BY ITS OWN
* FIXTURE. Measured 2026-09-08 with the key field named SID: the APPEND minted
* 3 and the arm read green. finalize_appended_record calls a SECOND generator,
* generate_sid_if_needed(), which asks no registry and no header and fires on
* any field literally NAMED "SID". The map was empty and the magic name minted
* the key. The arm was green and proved nothing.
*
* SO THE CONTROL NOW RIDES IN THE SAME ROW AS THE ARM. One APPEND writes both
* fields in one call: SID by name, EMPNO only if generation consults the stamp.
* Reading the pair:
*   G5 green, T3 red   -- THE ASYMMETRY IS REAL AND VISIBLE. Same row, same
*                         call: the undeclared field got a key and the declared,
*                         stamped one did not.
*   G5 green, T3 green -- generation reads the header too; the asymmetry is a
*                         code reading that does not manifest, and the #163 fix
*                         changes nothing observable.
*   G5 red             -- the control is dead. T3 is unattributable AGAIN and
*                         this is a fixture failure, not a finding.
*
* T3 AND T3B ARE COMPLEMENTARY READINGS OF ONE FIELD AND ONE OF THEM IS MEANT
* TO BE RED. Writing only the outcome I predict would make the prediction
* unfalsifiable. If BOTH read .F. the key holds some third value, and that is a
* finding rather than a broken arm.

APPEND

* PKD_G3 -- the APPEND landed on a NEW row. RECNO() and RECCOUNT() render EMPTY
* in a marker in this language, so the proof is a field read: records 1 and 2
* hold ALPHA and BETA and a fresh row holds neither. If the APPEND had been
* refused outright we would still be parked on record 1 and this reads .F.,
* which separates "APPEND refused" from "APPEND minted nothing" -- two findings
* the T3 pair alone cannot tell apart.
? "PKD_G3_undeclared_append_parked_on_a_blank_row:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "")

* PKD_G5 -- THE CONTROL. Nothing declares SID, here or in run 1, and the
* generator that fills it consults nothing. If this is red, generation did not
* run at all in this process and the arm below is measuring the wrong thing.
? "PKD_G5_undeclared_SID_minted_by_name:" + (SID = 3)

* PKD_T3 / PKD_T3B -- THE ARM. Same row, same APPEND, the field that IS declared
* and IS stamped in the header.
? "PKD_T3_stamped_key_minted_without_a_declaration:" + (EMPNO = 3)
? "PKD_T3B_stamped_key_left_at_zero:" + (EMPNO = 0)

* PKD_T4 -- and can a blank key be repaired by hand? 9 duplicates nothing
* whichever way T3 fell, so a refusal here refuses THE WRITE ITSELF and not a
* duplicate. Red here on top of a red T3 is the damaging combination: a row the
* engine will neither fill nor permit to be filled. Red here on top of a GREEN
* T3 is correct policy and not a defect -- a minted key is never edited, and
* the engine says so in as many words.
REPLACE EMPNO WITH "9"
? "PKD_T4_the_key_could_be_filled_by_hand:" + (EMPNO = 9)

* PKD_G4 -- the row is not inert. A refused key write and a row that accepts no
* write at all read identically at T4; this non-key write tells them apart.
* It runs AFTER T4 for the same reason PKP_G7 runs after PKP_T8 -- it can only
* prove its point by writing a field the other markers read.
REPLACE LNAME WITH "DELTA"
? "PKD_G4_the_new_row_accepts_a_non_key_write:" + (ALLTRIM(LNAME) = "DELTA")

CLOSE
ERASE TABLE PKDUR CONFIRM

FORMULA "PKDUR-RUN2-END"

SET ALTERNATE OFF
QUIT
